<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Westminster system</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Westminster parliamentary model, Westminster model</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Westminster system is the model of parliamentary government developed in the United Kingdom, in which the executive is drawn from and accountable to an elected legislature. It includes a ceremonial head of state, a prime minister and cabinet, and an official opposition. It was adopted by the self-governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> and many former colonies, making it one of the empire's most enduring legacies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/westminster-system.jpg" alt="Westminster system" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adrian Pingstone · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Houses.of.parliament.overall.arp.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The term derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palace-of-westminster">Palace of Westminster</a>, the seat of the British parliament. In the system, the head of state, usually a monarch or president, formally holds executive power but in practice acts on the advice of elected ministers. The head of government, called the prime minister, premier or chief minister, is the person who commands a majority in the lower house, and the head of state appoints that person to form a government. Executive authority is exercised by the cabinet, which is bound by collective responsibility, and the prime minister dominates it because cabinet members can be replaced or moved at any time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>A government that loses a vote of no confidence, or fails to pass an important bill such as the budget, must either resign or ask for a dissolution and new elections. Parties outside the government form the opposition and mirror the government with a shadow cabinet. Most Westminster parliaments have a lower house modelled on the House of Commons and a smaller upper house, chosen in different ways: by appointment, as in the Senate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, by direct election, as in the Australian Senate, or, until the House of Lords Act 1999, by hereditary right. Chambers are usually long rooms with government and opposition benches facing each other and a Speaker's Chair at one end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The system was used in the legislatures of most former colonies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> on gaining self-government, beginning with the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1848, and the British Parliament has been called the mother of parliaments. Many countries later changed course. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> abolished the model in its 1983 constitution, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>'s ended with the military coup of 1966, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>'s constitution of 1987 abolished it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> combines it with a powerful elected Senate, a blend nicknamed the Washminster mutation. The 1975 Australian constitutional crisis and the Canadian King–Byng affair of 1926 showed the reserve powers of the head of state in use.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Westminster system” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire left an extensive and durable legal inheritance: English common law, procedural traditions and constitutional frameworks were transplanted into dozens of successor states, and the <b>Westminster parliamentary model</b> was adopted, with varying success, across the former empire from India to Jamaica to Nigeria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The British North America Act of 1867 created a federation with a parliamentary system on the <b>Westminster model</b>: a Governor General representing the Crown, an elected House of Commons, an appointed Senate, and provinces with their own legislatures and powers over education, property and civil rights, while Quebec…</blockquote><a href="/empire/canada">Canada · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Until 1984 the republic kept a <b>Westminster-style</b> parliament elected by white voters only.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Rhodesia kept a <b>Westminster system</b> with a racial franchise.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rhodesia">Rhodesia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Singapore is a parliamentary republic on the <b>Westminster model</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Union was a parliamentary state on the <b>Westminster model</b>, with a governor-general representing the British Crown, a Senate, a House of Assembly and four provinces with elected councils.</blockquote><a href="/empire/union-of-south-africa">Union of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
